ng data from the constraint ablation and overhead analyses to populate T1 with the baseline and SICA performance across Qwen-14B, Qwen-7B, Qwen-72B-AWQ, and Mistral-7B. Looking at the GSM8K pilot results, the SICA approach shows a modest improvement over standard voting. Now examining the constraint quality metrics across 307 multi-candidate problems—precision averages around 0.57 with notable variation, and interestingly there's almost no correlation between precision and voting entropy. The constraint ablation study across 804 problems reveals that MaxSAT, count-only, random-weight, and standard voting all perform nearly identically around 0.76-0.77, with no statistically significant differences between them.\n\nFor tiebreaking, SICA substantially outperforms random and alphabetical approaches when candidates are tied. The temperature ablation shows an interesting pattern: lower temperatures keep more candidates in contention but don't improve performance, while higher temperatures dramatically increase the fraction of single-candidate problems and push SICA accuracy up to 0.817, though the extraction rate drops to 76%.\n\nNow looking at how the number of candidates affects performance, I'm seeing that SICA remains stable around 0.750 across K=6 through K=11, matching or slightly trailing the standard approach, but performance degrades as K increases further, with SICA dropping to 0.717 at K=16 and K=20 while tiebreaking becomes more frequent at smaller K values.\n\nFor the overhead analysis across 804 samples, trace generation and extraction dominate the runtime at roughly equal proportions around 50% each, with deduplication and MaxSAT contributing negligibly, and SICA itself accounting for about half the total overhead. The diagnostic on C0 shows two distinct behavioral clusters—cases with minimal constraint gaps cluster around zero while others show stronger positive gaps—though the linear relationship is weak. When I weight the standard approach by different 